          <p class="eyebrow">Child article</p>
          <h1>Speech delay red flags parents should notice</h1>
          <p class="lead">
            Parents often notice communication differences before anyone else.
            Careful observation can help you describe what is happening and
            decide whether a child-development assessment may help.
          </p>
<div class="blog-answer"><strong>In short:</strong> Parents often notice communication differences before anyone else.
            Careful observation can help you describe what is happening and
            decide whether a child-development assessment may help.</div>

        </section>
        <section class="surface panel">
          <h2>Look for patterns, not one isolated moment</h2>
          <ul class="icon-list">
            <li>Limited words compared with age expectations</li>
            <li>Difficulty understanding simple instructions</li>
            <li>Frustration because communication is hard</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
      </article>
    <section class="seo-section"><h2>Look at communication in everyday situations</h2><p>Speech and language develop at different rates, and one milestone does not tell the whole story. Notice how your child communicates needs, follows familiar instructions, shares attention, responds to people and joins play. Include gestures, pointing, facial expression, signs, pictures and other communication methods, not only spoken words. A child may understand more than they can say, or communicate differently with familiar people than in a new setting.</p><p>Write down examples from routines such as meals, getting dressed, play and school. Note what your child was trying to communicate, what happened before, how another person responded and whether the child seemed understood. A short video of an everyday interaction may be useful if the clinician asks for it, but do not delay an appointment to create a record.</p><h2>Consider the languages your child hears</h2><p>If your family uses more than one language, describe which people speak each language and what your child understands or says in each setting. A child may use different words with grandparents, caregivers, teachers or siblings. Multilingual exposure alone does not establish a communication disorder, and assessment should consider the languages and opportunities the child has had. Ask the clinician how information across languages will be gathered and interpreted.</p><h2>When to seek a conversation</h2><p>Ask a qualified professional when communication concerns persist, cause frustration or make it hard for your child to participate in routines. Mention promptly any loss of words, gestures or other communication skills the child previously used consistently. A loss of established skills deserves attention; do not wait for a checklist threshold before contacting a clinician. If hearing has not been checked and hearing is a concern, ask whether an audiology assessment is appropriate.</p><p>Assessment may include developmental and health history, observation, discussion with caregivers and information from school or other settings when available. No single online checklist can diagnose a child. A clinician can explain whether speech and language therapy, developmental assessment, hearing evaluation or another service is a suitable next step. For local care, see <a href="/aasha/speech-language-therapy">Aasha speech and language therapy</a> and <a href="/aasha/developmental-assessment">developmental assessment</a>. The <a href="/aasha/developmental-check-in">developmental check-in</a> is a discussion aid, not a diagnostic or validated screening test.</p><h2>Prepare a few practical questions</h2><ul><li>What can my child currently understand and communicate in different routines?</li><li>Would hearing, developmental or psychological assessment help answer the question?</li><li>How will the therapist include the languages used at home and school?</li><li>What goals could support communication and participation in daily life?</li><li>How will we review progress and discuss the current fee and visit frequency?</li></ul><p>All child-related care is coordinated through Aasha Early Intervention Centre. Use <a href="/aasha/book">Aasha appointment requests</a> to ask which first visit fits your concern. A request does not confirm a slot. If your child has an urgent medical need, contact emergency services or the nearest emergency department rather than waiting for a routine appointment.</p></section>
    <section class="seo-section"><h2>Explore support at Aasha</h2><p>Learn about <a href="/aasha/speech-language-therapy">speech and language therapy at Aasha in Greater Noida</a>, or <a href="/aasha#contact">contact the team to discuss an assessment</a>.</p></section>
